    "created_at": "2021-09-06T20:43:39Z",
    "updated_at": "2021-09-06T20:43:39Z",
    "body": "> I'd be slightly concerned about distributing TOML files with the project since this would represent a packaging change, and we have a lot of different packages out there, not all of which are under our direct control.\r\n\r\nMy solution here wasn’t assuming they’d ship with the binary, just that users could copy the TOML from the website. My original motivation here was “I don’t have any good way of telling when the presets are updated or what the diffs are”.\r\n\r\nIf you do want to ship them, there’s two options that come to mind:\r\n1. Do the first alternate and have me just declare what presets I want. This would likely be an array of preset names, to control the order if they conflict. The presets themselves would be baked into the binary rather than be standalone files.\r\n2. Do my original suggestion but also teach the binary how to print presets. This could be a new flag to `starship print-config`, such as `starship print-config --preset` to list the available ones and `starship print-config --preset $name` to print the preset. I could then pipe the preset I want to a file and use the suggested include mechanism.\r\n\r\nThe second option seems the most powerful and flexible. I can have the generic include mechanism for modularity (including being able to create and distribute my own presets), and I can use the flag to generate the known presets in order to easily keep them up-to-date. This would especially work as I use Nix to install starship and so I can teach it to generate the presets during installation so they’re always up-to-date.",
    "author_association": "NONE",
    "pin": null,
